  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA surface low will start to deepen off the coast of the\nCarolinas during the day on Sunday before riding up the coast\nthrough Monday. The heavy rain band associated with this low\npressure system will be fairly narrow with lighter rain\nsurrounding it. There are still significant differences between\nthe placement of the band in the various deterministics and\nensemble members. Some keep QPF an inch or less, even on the\ncoastal plain. Others are farther inland with a general 1 to 2\ninches for a large portion of the forecast area. A definitive\ntrend has not yet been established so uncertainty is still \nquite high in exact amounts and if this will be a much needed \nsoaking rain for a lot of folks. Will continue to monitor \ntrends. \n\nA cold front will cross the forecast area early in the day on\nMonday will will lead to winds shifting from out of the\nsoutheast to out of the northwest, becoming breezy by the \nafternoon hours. GFS soundings suggest the potential for some\nsites to see 25 to 35 knots at the top of the mixed layer.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nThe active pattern may well continue into mid week towards the \nThanksgiving holiday as long range guidance continues to signal \nthe chance for additional low pressure systems organizing in the\neastern CONUS and moving up the coast. Solutions are even wider\nwith this proposition, so confidence is low, but this will \noverlap with the monthly high astronomical tide cycle. The high \ntides will make the coast susceptible to coastal flooding if \ncombined with swell and surge from a coastal low. Those with \ninterests along the coast should monitor coming forecasts should\nthis threat become more likely.",
